Ten years ago, almost everybody had Java installed
(because a lot of websites required it), so releasing
something that required Java was indeed a fairly
reasonable way of ensuring everyone would be
able to use it.  Today, Java has been almost totally
obviated by Flash[1], and so almost nobody has it
installed on their computers any more.  Additionally,
the new owners have made it increasingly difficult
to figure out which thing to download if you want to
run Java programs[2], so you can no longer just tell
people "to run this you need to install Java".  You
have to give specific instructions and a link for
exactly what "Java" to download.  From the
perspective of trying to find a distribution medium
that a lot of people can use easily, Java is now a
much less attractive option than it used to be.
(It's a much _better_ option than it used to be
from a performance perspective, but the world
is full of such irony.)

> However, releasing the source in a non-proprietary
